# Does the SORTING kernel still pay on the FULL 8-dimensional design?
#
# `sorting-rbf` is now the default in config/enzyme.yaml, but it was only ever MEASURED on the
# shared-fraction design (scripts/benchmark_symmetry.py: 1 + n coordinates, 5 seeds x 30 iterations,
# 1.29x over the null at p = 0.016). There the exchangeable element is a SCALAR temperature, and
# sorting is then exactly the quotient metric -- the rearrangement inequality gives
# ||sort a - sort b|| = min_pi ||a - pi b||, so the folded distance IS the distance between the two
# sets. That identity is what made the arm defensible.
#
# On the FULL design the element is the PAIR (fraction, temperature). Sorting by temperature carries
# each experiment's fraction along, which is exactly invariant and PSD -- it is a composition, not a
# minimisation -- but it is a KEYED invariant, not the quotient metric: the permutation it picks
# agrees with the distance-minimising assignment only ~25% of the time (24.4% over 20000 pairs) on random pairs. So the
# earlier result does NOT transfer, and a default that rests on it is a default resting on a
# measurement of a different function. This is the campaign that measures it where it is used.
#
# ONE surrogate arm, by design. Which kernel to use is settled -- sorting -- so this is not a kernel
# comparison and does not spend seeds on the alternatives; it is the acceptance check for the arm
# that was chosen, on the design it is actually configured over. (An 8-seed `ard-rbf` run at these
# same settings exists in output/kernel8-partial-ard-only, a by-product of an earlier aborted
# 4-arm launch. It is not part of THIS campaign and `acceptance` run over THIS directory does not
# see it -- but it was later scored against this campaign's null in output/kernel8-4arm, which is
# legitimate because the resolved settings match. An earlier revision of this line said the analysis
# "does not read it" full stop; that became false the moment the 4-arm comparison was assembled.)
#
# HOW TO READ THE p-VALUE THIS PRODUCES. `acceptance` reports one BO-vs-null test per arm, and at
# these settings THREE such tests (sort, pi, ard) share ONE set of ten null runs, so they are neither
# independent nor multiplicity-corrected. Holm over the three: pi 0.041, sort 0.101, ard 0.76 -- so
# sort's raw one-sided 0.034 does NOT survive correction, and the two-sided raw value is 0.068 to
# begin with. Treat a single arm's p here as a screening number, not as a result. (The `w2` arm in
# output/wide is NOT in this family: it is the 2% read-out, a different objective with its own null.)
#
# Everything else is config/detector/enzyme.yaml AS IT IS NOW: T in [25, 80], m = 4, 5% read-out.
# That box is the one whose iteration payoff differentiates BO from the null (1.16x); the earlier
# kernel comparison in output/kernel ran the OLD [35, 68] box, so its numbers are about a different
# admissible set and are not carried over here.
sort |bo    |--kernel sorting-rbf
null |random|
